<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0506">Example <span data-tag="0506">1.1.4.15</span>.</a> When $n=2$, Proposition <a data-tag="0504" href="/tag/0504">1.1.4.13</a> asserts that morphisms of simplicial sets $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^2 \rightarrow S$ can be identified with ordered triples $(g, h, f)$ of edges of $S$ having the property that $f$ and $h$ have the same source vertex $x \in S$, $g$ and $h$ have the same target vertex $z \in S$, and the target $y$ of $f$ coincides with the source of $g$; these relationships are summarized visually in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ x \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  z. } \]
</div>
</article>
